.TH KULA 1 "November 2026" "kula" "User Manuals"
.SH NAME
kula \- lightweight system monitoring daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kula
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR] [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kula
(Kula) is a self-contained, lightweight system monitoring daemon written in Go.
It collects system metrics (CPU, memory, disk, network) and provides a web interface
and a local terminal UI (TUI) for visualization.
.SH COMMANDS
If no command is specified, \fBserve\fR is used by default.
.TP
.B serve
Starts the data collection daemon and web server.
.TP
.B tui
Starts the local Terminal User Interface (TUI) monitor. Its History view charts the
data recorded by \fBserve\fR, reading the storage tier files read-only.
.TP
.B hash-password
Generates an Argon2id hash of a password, suitable for use in the configuration file.
.TP
.B inspect
Displays storage tier resolution, coverage, and file information.
Pass \fB\-\-verbose\fR to also print the latest recorded metrics from each tier.
A tier file that does not exist yet, as on a fresh install, is reported and is not an
error. One that exists but cannot be inspected is reported on standard error, and
\fBkula\fR exits with status 1 after printing the other tiers.
.TP
.B disks
Lists available disks and partitions supported by Kula, with their persistent IDs.
Copy IDs into collection.devices to select disks across reboots.
Devices without a unique persistent ID are marked unavailable (unstable kernel name).
This command does not load configuration or access storage; a \fB\-config\fR given with it
is ignored, and no configuration file is created. Configured device filters do not
restrict the listing. Virtual, logical and optical devices excluded by Kula's disk
collector are omitted.
.SH OPTIONS
\fB\-config\fR, \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-\-help\fR may be given before the command or after it;
\fB\-v\fR and \fB\-version\fR only before it. Every option may be written with one dash or
two, and an option after the command takes precedence over the same option before it.
.TP
\fB\-config\fR \fIFILE\fP
Path to the configuration file (default: \fIconfig.yaml\fR in the working directory).
Without this option, a missing \fIconfig.yaml\fR means the built-in defaults. A \fIFILE\fR
given explicitly is always used: if it does not exist, it is created from the packaged
example configuration, with mode 0600, and a warning is logged; if it cannot be created
there or cannot be read, \fBkula\fR refuses to start rather than run on built-in defaults,
which would serve without authentication. A setting the configuration does not know, such
as a misspelled key, also stops \fBkula\fR, with an error naming its line.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show the help message and exit. After a command, show that command's help; it then reads
no configuration and starts nothing.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-version\fR
Print the version and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-verbose\fR
After \fBinspect\fR only: also print the latest recorded metrics from each tier.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
These variables override the corresponding settings of the configuration file:
.TP
.B KULA_DIRECTORY
storage.directory, where the tier files are written.
.TP
.B KULA_LOGLEVEL
web.logging.level: access, perf or debug. Set but empty, it disables logging.
.TP
.B KULA_LISTEN
web.listen, the bind address.
.TP
.B KULA_PORT
web.port, the bind port. A value outside 1\-65535 is ignored with a warning.
.TP
.B KULA_UNIX_SOCKET
web.unix_socket: listen on a Unix socket instead of TCP.
.TP
.B KULA_MOUNTS_DETECTION
collection.mounts_detection: auto, host or self.
.TP
.B KULA_BASE_PATH
web.base_path, the URL sub-path prefix. Set but empty, it clears the configured one.
.TP
.B KULA_POSTGRES_PASSWORD
applications.postgres.password.
.TP
.B KULA_MYSQL_PASSWORD
applications.mysql.password.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, or the help or the version was printed.
.TP
.B 1
An error: an unknown command, a configuration or storage that cannot be used, or, for
\fBinspect\fR, a tier file that exists but cannot be inspected.
.TP
.B 2
A command line \fBkula\fR does not accept: an unknown option, or an argument the command
does not take.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/kula/config.yaml
Default system-wide configuration file.
.TP
.I /var/lib/kula
Default directory for persistent data storage.
